+// The order of the GPU work across the two processes. Direct3D 11 asks a writer to call Flush before another device
+// uses a texture that they share without a keyed mutex. After that the reader sees the writes. Metal orders no work
+// of two processes on a shared texture: before cranvr's DXMT patch 0022 the second process could copy a texture
+// before the first process's GPU had cleared it (cycle 13 of 200 in one run read zeros of rgba16f). To show such a
+// gap at once, each process keeps its GPU busy before its clears with some copies of a texture of 4096 x 4096. The
+// clears wait on the GPU for that work (the busy work's own fence). The first process clears the textures, flushes and
+// sends the handles at once. It does not wait for its GPU. The second process then reads the colors while the first
+// GPU may still copy. It also writes back: it clears bgra8 to another color after its own busy work, flushes and
+// replies. The first process reads that color at once. Each wrong color fails the run.
